WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.532
- Welcome everyone, those here at Yale

00:00:07.532 --> 00:00:09.270
participating in person

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:13.370
and the many more that are
participating via zoom.

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:15.330
My name is Claudia Valeggia,

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.160
I am a professor of Anthropology

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.070
here at Yale and the Chair of the council

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:22.380
on Latin American Liberian studies.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.920
And it is a great pleasure

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:29.920
to introducing you to our guest today.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:32.817
So, I am delighted to welcome you,

00:00:32.817 --> 00:00:34.860
and our distinguished guest,

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:37.660
to the first event of the Latin American

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:41.270
Policy leader series.

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:43.220
The series, and this is a mouthful,

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:45.210
the series is generously supported

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:48.400
by The George Herbert
Walker Junior Lecture Fund

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.250
in International Studies
at the MacMillan Center

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:53.950
for International Area Studies.

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:55.740
I said it all in one.

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:58.666
(class laughs)

00:00:58.666 --> 00:01:02.570
First, a brief thanks, should be longer,

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:04.930
but a brief thanks to
the many people involved

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:09.260
in organizing today's
event, our team at CLAIS,

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:14.260
particularly Agustina and Asia
and Josh for being wonderful,

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:20.840
a wonderful team supporting this event.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:23.290
Then the Fox International
Fellowship Program,

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:25.290
the program on peace and development

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:27.160
at the Yale MacMillan Center

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:31.120
and the Office of International
Affairs, thank you all.

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:35.440
Before I introduce our
speaker and moderator today,

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:36.930
I want to share a few words

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:40.603
about our Latin American
Policy Leader Series.

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:44.580
Latin America is a home
to some of the world's

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:49.320
largest democracies and the
region has made enormous strides

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:52.750
in consolidating democratic
norms and human rights

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:54.683
since the end of the cold war.

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:58.260
The Latin American Policy Leader Series

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:01.160
seeks to create dialogue about the future

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:04.970
of democratization, human
rights and development

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:07.990
with policy leaders
from across the region.

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:10.530
Our hope is to engage these leaders

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:15.530
in vigorous and respectful
discussion about pressing issues.

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:19.500
As part of the series, we
are pleased to be welcoming

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.360
the former Environmental
Ministry of Ecuador

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:25.210
and President of the World
Wildlife Fund International,

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:30.210
Yolanda Kakabadse on March
11th, so mark your calendars.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:35.010
On April 19th, we will host a
discussion with the President

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:38.130
of the Inter-American
Court of Human Rights,

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:40.440
Ricardo Pérez Manrique.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:43.500
And on May 20th, we
will wrap up the series

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.870
with Maria-Noel Vaeza, Regional Director

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:50.263
of Latin American and the
Caribbean for the UN Women.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:55.120
Now, regarding today's
event and our speaker,

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:58.860
I am delighted to introduce
the Secretary General

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:03.160
of the organization of American
states, Dr. Luis Almagro,

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:05.170
who is originally from Uruguay

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:07.490
and is a lawyer by profession.

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:09.800
He's currently serving his second term

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:14.200
as Secretary General of the
Organization of American states.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:17.184
The Secretary General was
Uruguay's Foreign Minister

00:03:17.184 --> 00:03:22.184
between 2010 and 2015,
during his tenure Uruguay

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:25.744
welcomed former prisoner from Guantanamo,

00:03:25.744 --> 00:03:29.300
granted asylum to dozens
of Syrian families,

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:31.980
who were victims of civil war,

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:35.023
and secure endorsement of
the group of Latin American

00:03:35.023 --> 00:03:39.460
Caribbean countries ruler,
for its election to a seat

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:43.463
on the United Nations Security
Council in January, 2016.

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:48.630
He was also ambassador
to China for three years

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:51.420
after occupying senior diplomatic posts

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:53.680
in his country's foreign ministry,

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:56.443
and its embassies in Germany and Iran.

00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:59.828
In addition, he was elected as a Senator

00:03:59.828 --> 00:04:04.743
in Uruguay's national
elections in October 2014.

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:08.960
Luis Almagro was first
elected as Secretary General

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:13.080
of the OAS in March 18th, 2015,

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:16.410
with a support of 33
of the 34 member states

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:18.210
and one abstention.

00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:22.893
He was reelected for a
second term on May 20, 2020.

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:28.130
After the Secretary's general remarks,

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:31.320
our class faculty member
professor Ana De La O,

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:35.060
we'll sit up in our living room here

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:38.190
for discussion before
opening up for questions

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:40.700
and answer sessions
with those in this room,

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:44.263
and also attendees that
are joining us virtually.

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:47.730
Ana De La O is a professor
in the Department

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:50.150
of Political Science here at Yale,

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:53.200
and is also the Director
of Undergraduate Studies

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:56.850
for Latin American studies,
which is our signature major,

00:04:56.850 --> 00:05:01.630
and those of you who are
considering majors as students,

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:06.630
please contact Ana or
CLAIS for more information.

00:05:07.446 --> 00:05:09.910
She's also affiliated with the Institute

00:05:09.910 --> 00:05:11.837
of Social and Policy studies

00:05:11.837 --> 00:05:14.960
and the Jackson School of Global Affairs.

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:18.240
Her research plays to
the political economy

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.093
of poverty alleviation, clientalism,

00:05:21.093 --> 00:05:23.313
and the provisions of public words.

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:27.940
Professor De La O is the
author of numerous articles

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:29.830
in journals, such as the American journal

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:32.470
of Political Science and
the Journal of Politics,

00:05:32.470 --> 00:05:35.297
as well as the book, "Crafting Policies

00:05:35.297 --> 00:05:37.670
"to End Poverty in Latin America."

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:38.963
A must read.

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:42.280
She teaches courses on
the political economy

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:46.260
of poverty alleviation,
paradigms of political economy,

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:48.260
the politics of redistribution

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:50.900
and quantitative research methods.

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:53.380
Thank you so much, Ana,
for lending your expertise

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:56.500
and moderating our conversation today.

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:58.129
I understand the Secretary General

00:05:58.129 --> 00:06:02.650
will make some brief
remarks, so please listen in

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:07.100
and be ready with any questions
for the Q and A session.

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:09.883
And I want to add a
personal anecdote here,

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:16.010
I am here, presiding this event
in part thanks to the OAS.

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:22.830
I came to the United States to do my PhD

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:27.830
with funding from the OAS,
alone, that will be justly paid

00:06:27.984 --> 00:06:32.984
(crowd laughter) on monthly
installments that allowed us

00:06:33.162 --> 00:06:37.910
to pursue our degree without
having to work outside.

00:06:37.910 --> 00:06:39.540
So thank you to the OAS.

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:44.770
So without further ado, please
please join me in welcoming

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:48.090
Secretary General of OAS, Luis Almagro.

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:50.590
(crowd claps)

00:07:05.707 --> 00:07:09.360
- Thank you very much to all of you.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:11.093
It's a great pleasure to be here.

00:07:12.365 --> 00:07:14.310
The first thing in
democracy, in Latin America

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:16.933
and the Caribbean progress
and peace development.

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:22.650
If we see the hemisphere, of course,

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:24.440
we may see some serious challenges

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:29.373
related to the state of
democracy in some countries.

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:32.790
The conditions for progress

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:35.893
and development, always uncertain,

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:41.880
and the conditions of these are
related to security matters.

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:42.930
And of course,

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:45.630
the challenges that we
have in the hemisphere.

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:48.773
So all this means that we have,

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:54.910
I will have to enlist some
failures related to all this.

00:07:54.910 --> 00:07:59.083
As you may imagine, democracy
is not always so healthy.

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:02.070
Security is not always available

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:04.483
for all Latin Americans, Caribbean people.

00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:09.843
Development is always
uncertain and then we fear,

00:08:09.843 --> 00:08:14.843
we need to do something
else in order to jump out

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:19.093
of the trap of underdevelopment
that we always are.

00:08:20.230 --> 00:08:25.230
Of course, for me, there
is an obvious conclusion

00:08:26.670 --> 00:08:30.320
looking at the hemisphere,
the better the democracy,

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:32.510
the better the conditions for development,

00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:35.710
the better are the
conditions for security,

00:08:35.710 --> 00:08:38.303
better conditions for
access to rights and equity.

00:08:40.130 --> 00:08:45.130
Democracy, it means
best possible citizens,

00:08:45.290 --> 00:08:48.590
best possible institutions
are as strong as possible,

00:08:48.590 --> 00:08:51.640
efficient institutions that
can deliver to the people,

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:53.680
that can provide to the people,

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:57.270
that can find solutions
for the structural problems

00:08:57.270 --> 00:08:58.850
of the people.

00:08:58.850 --> 00:09:03.850
Of course, we have some
structural problems.

00:09:04.131 --> 00:09:06.607
Some of them, they have been with us

00:09:06.607 --> 00:09:09.583
for 200 years of independent life.

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:15.660
I think, of course we have
also new ones, old grievances,

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:21.113
new grievances, old goons, new ones.

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:26.160
We have a structural
problem, the past 200 years,

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:30.213
we are the most unequal
region in the world.

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:34.640
And this is not just means inequality

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:39.640
in the context of wealth distribution,

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:43.470
but also access to rights,
we are very unequal

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:46.750
how people access to
right in our hemisphere.

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:48.660
We still discriminate a lot,

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:51.400
we still have a lot of serious challenges.

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:56.160
In order to resolve the
conditions for everybody

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:01.160
to have a fair life and
opportunities in the hemisphere.

00:10:02.420 --> 00:10:07.030
We are facing serious
problems related to this.

00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:11.090
Inequality, it affects our democracies,

00:10:11.090 --> 00:10:16.090
but it affects also our
levels of development.

00:10:18.470 --> 00:10:20.340
A better democracy, and of course,

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:23.010
we need to have better
conditions of equality

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:26.260
in the hemisphere, better
access to right for people,

00:10:26.260 --> 00:10:31.220
and to be able from there
to have better conditions

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:32.620
for development.

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:35.520
If we think that inequality
is just a political issue,

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:39.740
we are wrong, inequality is
an issue, cross cutting issue,

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:42.120
that affects practically every level

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:46.113
of the life that we
live in the hemisphere.

00:10:47.030 --> 00:10:50.773
We have, of course, a
lot of challenges ahead,

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:55.793
we have to fix this.

00:10:57.790 --> 00:11:01.210
We have not worked it hard
in the past 200 years,

00:11:01.210 --> 00:11:04.073
we have a big problem about that.

00:11:06.190 --> 00:11:10.130
I always mention the case of the rules

00:11:10.130 --> 00:11:13.615
for the land reform of Artigas, 1815,

00:11:13.615 --> 00:11:15.833
that refers that (speaks Spanish),

00:11:19.870 --> 00:11:24.630
those unhappy, those most vulnerable

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:27.970
to be the most privileged
in the context of politics.

00:11:27.970 --> 00:11:32.970
And he identified the
main vulnerable groups

00:11:34.765 --> 00:11:38.780
in what it was the one that entailed them.

00:11:38.780 --> 00:11:43.390
And they were with children,

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:45.940
they were Afro descendant population,

00:11:45.940 --> 00:11:47.340
they were Indigenous people.

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:51.260
200 years later, we have the same pattern

00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:56.260
of poverty in the country,
we have single mothers

00:11:56.360 --> 00:12:01.360
count for a lot of the poverty
of the region of the country.

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:08.570
Afro descendant people, they
count for 8% of the population

00:12:08.570 --> 00:12:12.470
and for 40% of the poverty
and Indigenous people,

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:16.707
they were terminated immediately
after the independence,

00:12:17.620 --> 00:12:19.890
so they don't count anymore.

00:12:19.890 --> 00:12:24.280
That means we really have some
serious structural problems

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:26.420
because that, that is considered

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:31.290
the most equal country
among Latino Americans,

00:12:31.290 --> 00:12:35.650
is still the one that,
it still have the same,

00:12:35.650 --> 00:12:39.100
very same structural
problems that it has had

00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:40.043
for quite a while.

00:12:41.925 --> 00:12:46.925
We are supposed to come
from very liberal ideas.

00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:51.210
Our independence, our constitutions,

00:12:51.210 --> 00:12:54.383
they were like, they have
a democratic vocation,

00:12:55.250 --> 00:12:59.710
most of them, they took the Thomas Paine,

00:12:59.710 --> 00:13:02.440
and they took the principles
of the American Revolution,

00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:03.763
of the French Revolution.

00:13:05.930 --> 00:13:10.900
But when we scratch it a
little bit, just a little bit,

00:13:10.900 --> 00:13:15.560
we know that we are a highly
paternalistic, we are unequal,

00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:19.573
we have patterns of racists
practically everywhere.

00:13:20.699 --> 00:13:25.699
We have this poverty, structural
poverty in the hemisphere.

00:13:26.330 --> 00:13:29.990
So, we still have a lot to do

00:13:29.990 --> 00:13:33.960
to put this liberal
mind, this liberal ideas,

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:37.600
these advanced constitutions
and legislation into practice,

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:40.060
to bring solutions for the real people.

00:13:40.060 --> 00:13:45.060
That it depends where they
were born and the class,

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:51.023
the neighborhood, and the
region in the country,

00:13:52.010 --> 00:13:54.070
and of course, race and gender

00:13:54.070 --> 00:13:58.230
and other levels of
inequality and discrimination

00:13:58.230 --> 00:13:59.483
that exist in the region.

00:14:00.610 --> 00:14:03.720
And of course, this all brings patterns

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:06.010
of exclusion and polarization.

00:14:06.010 --> 00:14:09.920
And I am presuming the
problems in order that we,

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:12.300
those are the things
that we need to address.

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:15.070
We need to address this in order
to have better democracies,

00:14:15.070 --> 00:14:17.090
we need to address this in order

00:14:17.090 --> 00:14:21.523
to have a better
possibility for development.

00:14:23.460 --> 00:14:26.360
Exclusion and polarization,
it comes with this,

00:14:27.486 --> 00:14:31.500
with what I have just said
of the levels of inequality,

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:35.663
of this crosscut and
inequality in our countries.

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:45.470
We have in the past years,

00:14:45.470 --> 00:14:50.470
we even moved to further
polarization in our countries.

00:14:52.370 --> 00:14:57.370
Sometimes, we have seen in
the region two projects,

00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:04.320
impossible to reconciliate
between each other,

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:06.853
passing to the second round of elections.

00:15:08.180 --> 00:15:13.180
And maybe sometimes because citizens say,

00:15:13.927 --> 00:15:18.110
"Look for radical approach
to for solutions."

00:15:18.110 --> 00:15:21.350
Sometimes on the one
side or the other side,

00:15:21.350 --> 00:15:25.957
sometimes because we are just
losing the common ground,

00:15:29.860 --> 00:15:33.620
sometimes because we are
not able to handle properly

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:36.613
the political discussion,
the political participation

00:15:36.613 --> 00:15:38.520
that come with social media,

00:15:38.520 --> 00:15:40.447
we have all these challenges ahead.

00:15:40.447 --> 00:15:45.447
It has been very hard for
us to develop a substantial

00:15:46.840 --> 00:15:50.130
and constructive and positive agenda

00:15:50.130 --> 00:15:52.300
based on our political discussions.

00:15:52.300 --> 00:15:56.500
And we have been falling
apart in these discussions

00:15:56.500 --> 00:15:59.004
becoming more and more paralyzed.

00:15:59.004 --> 00:16:04.004
And not only paralyzations,
it goes sometimes

00:16:04.610 --> 00:16:08.290
to the immunization of
the political adversity,

00:16:08.290 --> 00:16:10.213
what is even more irrational,

00:16:14.002 --> 00:16:16.050
and that brings another problem.

00:16:16.050 --> 00:16:20.290
That is, you remain in
power no matter what.

00:16:20.290 --> 00:16:24.890
And that remaining in
power is always the base

00:16:24.890 --> 00:16:29.890
for some people, of course,
to get some immunity,

00:16:30.380 --> 00:16:33.980
impunity, or amnesty,
and it has been proved

00:16:33.980 --> 00:16:38.760
in this hemisphere that
no dictator is safe

00:16:38.760 --> 00:16:42.640
from the long memory of
human rights justice,

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:45.890
or the long arm of human rights justice.

00:16:45.890 --> 00:16:49.251
So that the only way
dictators remain impugned

00:16:49.251 --> 00:16:52.130
is because they remain in power.

00:16:52.130 --> 00:16:53.920
That is the only way for amnesty,

00:16:53.920 --> 00:16:57.030
that is the only way for
immunity, for impunity.

00:16:57.030 --> 00:16:59.290
The impunity that comes
from remaining in power,

00:16:59.290 --> 00:17:01.670
whatever the cost may be.

00:17:01.670 --> 00:17:04.620
And we have seen in that sense,

00:17:04.620 --> 00:17:07.550
that we pass from one
dictatorship in the hemisphere

00:17:07.550 --> 00:17:12.110
to three dictatorships
in some four, five years,

00:17:12.110 --> 00:17:16.300
six, seven years, maybe some
process were more accelerated,

00:17:16.300 --> 00:17:21.120
Some process were more
gradual, but it went that way.

00:17:21.120 --> 00:17:25.593
Looking for these conditions
of impunity, of immunity.

00:17:27.410 --> 00:17:30.581
And that of course are the challenges

00:17:30.581 --> 00:17:35.581
of dealing also with zeros sum mentality,

00:17:35.750 --> 00:17:40.750
that is taking a lot from us.

00:17:42.550 --> 00:17:45.690
It's like, we are not able to find the way

00:17:45.690 --> 00:17:50.690
that we can work together, but
how we can defeat the other

00:17:52.000 --> 00:17:55.373
and how we can take
advantage of that victory.

00:17:56.360 --> 00:18:01.360
We have to, we are looking for
relevance in the wrong way.

00:18:03.247 --> 00:18:06.950
We need to look for relevance
based on the solutions

00:18:06.950 --> 00:18:08.740
that we find for our people,

00:18:08.740 --> 00:18:10.970
how we eliminate discrimination,

00:18:10.970 --> 00:18:14.180
how we eliminate poverty,
how we eliminate inequality,

00:18:14.180 --> 00:18:17.210
how we create better
conditions for the people

00:18:17.210 --> 00:18:19.770
to integrate and to
consolidate a common ground

00:18:19.770 --> 00:18:23.010
that may allow institutions
to work in the long term.

00:18:23.010 --> 00:18:24.810
To execute plans in the long term,

00:18:24.810 --> 00:18:26.803
to design plans in the long term.

00:18:28.170 --> 00:18:31.770
All this, it means sometimes
going back to square one,

00:18:31.770 --> 00:18:33.660
institutions going back to square one,

00:18:33.660 --> 00:18:35.840
political system going back to square one,

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:40.140
or just holding to
power in no matter what,

00:18:40.140 --> 00:18:42.840
no matter how bad is
your humanitarian crisis,

00:18:42.840 --> 00:18:45.500
no matter how bad is immigration crisis,

00:18:45.500 --> 00:18:48.110
no matter how bad is your corruption,

00:18:48.110 --> 00:18:51.360
no matter how bad are your
human rights violations,

00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:52.910
just remain in power.

00:18:52.910 --> 00:18:57.840
And that school of thought,
regrettably is something

00:18:57.840 --> 00:19:00.960
that we also have in this hemisphere,

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:03.723
also have among Latino Americans.

00:19:07.360 --> 00:19:12.360
What we do, we deal
with political systems,

00:19:12.870 --> 00:19:15.790
we try to bring stability and we try

00:19:15.790 --> 00:19:17.750
to strengthen the democratic institutions.

00:19:17.750 --> 00:19:22.630
We try to create conditions
for transparent government,

00:19:22.630 --> 00:19:25.320
we try to create the conditions
for the better projects

00:19:25.320 --> 00:19:28.240
that can bring and develop
according to the pillars

00:19:28.240 --> 00:19:31.733
of the organization, democracy,
access to right security.

00:19:35.250 --> 00:19:40.250
The thing is that this
is a serious challenge,

00:19:41.080 --> 00:19:44.410
And we, you expect an organization

00:19:44.410 --> 00:19:49.240
with not enough financial resources,

00:19:49.240 --> 00:19:54.060
not enough human resources
to fix these four pillars

00:19:54.060 --> 00:19:58.160
in a practically, a hundred
percent efficient way.

00:19:58.160 --> 00:20:00.840
And in a context, of course,

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:03.630
that we have today of conflict,

00:20:03.630 --> 00:20:07.233
of social and political
conflict escalation,

00:20:08.230 --> 00:20:12.409
but the solution is this, the OAS,

00:20:12.409 --> 00:20:17.409
it means materialists, and
that it means means stability.

00:20:18.510 --> 00:20:22.070
That is what, how we work,
how we create conditions

00:20:22.070 --> 00:20:24.920
that can bring better
democratic governments.

00:20:24.920 --> 00:20:29.120
And that is how we push more or less,

00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:32.730
most of the time we can
be an immortal force,

00:20:32.730 --> 00:20:35.420
and that is the thing of the organization.

00:20:35.420 --> 00:20:39.900
I mean, our projects cannot have the scale

00:20:39.900 --> 00:20:44.900
of financial institutions, but
we are a strong moral force

00:20:45.180 --> 00:20:49.390
in creating conditions that
can help some governments

00:20:49.390 --> 00:20:52.600
and democratic governments,
and democratic institutions

00:20:52.600 --> 00:20:56.910
to verbalize and to face
the threats that they have,

00:20:56.910 --> 00:20:59.430
the threats that they
have that are related

00:20:59.430 --> 00:21:04.170
to the existential threats
that we have in the hemisphere.

00:21:04.170 --> 00:21:07.560
And I go to Caribbean and
Central American countries.

00:21:07.560 --> 00:21:12.250
For example, first of one,
the one that is challenging

00:21:12.250 --> 00:21:15.340
their own existence is climate change.

00:21:15.340 --> 00:21:20.340
I mean, we have a group of
small islands that year by year,

00:21:20.440 --> 00:21:24.560
are devastated by hurricanes,
two, three countries,

00:21:24.560 --> 00:21:28.960
in like Caribbean countries,
Central American countries,

00:21:28.960 --> 00:21:32.080
they deeply affected, but what is going on

00:21:32.080 --> 00:21:33.310
in climate change in the world?

00:21:33.310 --> 00:21:35.560
And for us, climate
change is not a rhetoric,

00:21:35.560 --> 00:21:38.770
it's not a speech, it is something

00:21:38.770 --> 00:21:43.380
that it brings a serious
impact to million of people,

00:21:43.380 --> 00:21:45.790
thousands of people, every, every year.

00:21:45.790 --> 00:21:49.248
We are the first frontier
of climate change.

00:21:49.248 --> 00:21:52.590
Practically, no other
region has in the world,

00:21:52.590 --> 00:21:55.920
has this impact, serious impact,

00:21:55.920 --> 00:21:57.743
every year that we are facing.

00:22:00.940 --> 00:22:05.863
The other existential threat is security.

00:22:06.750 --> 00:22:09.710
We have a serious challenge there

00:22:09.710 --> 00:22:12.540
and something that we
definitely need to address.

00:22:12.540 --> 00:22:15.310
We are 8% of the world population,

00:22:15.310 --> 00:22:18.500
we count for 78% of violent death.

00:22:18.500 --> 00:22:23.500
Violent death, and we have 15 of the 20

00:22:24.030 --> 00:22:28.140
most violent cities in the world.

00:22:28.140 --> 00:22:33.140
We are the definitely
far to deliver solutions

00:22:33.450 --> 00:22:38.450
from the crisis and the
terror that organized crime

00:22:40.100 --> 00:22:42.320
and drug trafficking is bringing

00:22:42.320 --> 00:22:44.493
to our countries in the hemisphere.

00:22:45.410 --> 00:22:50.300
We have not been able to reverse that,

00:22:50.300 --> 00:22:54.360
I think, we need stronger and
more efficient institutions

00:22:54.360 --> 00:22:58.940
that definitely each
country can hardly have.

00:22:58.940 --> 00:23:03.940
If you look at the budget of
drug trafficking organization,

00:23:04.100 --> 00:23:06.233
compared to what may have,

00:23:07.720 --> 00:23:12.720
you know, police station,
some where in our countries,

00:23:12.770 --> 00:23:15.150
definitely they are far behind

00:23:15.150 --> 00:23:20.150
and they have far stronger,
harder challenges than anybody.

00:23:24.007 --> 00:23:29.007
We need international
cooperation for that.

00:23:29.220 --> 00:23:34.220
We need a permanent engagement
of developed countries,

00:23:34.530 --> 00:23:37.510
bringing better conditions
of security to our countries.

00:23:37.510 --> 00:23:39.880
And of course, a better
regional cooperation

00:23:39.880 --> 00:23:41.732
related to this matter.

00:23:41.732 --> 00:23:46.180
And that at a certain moment
that we can depend ourself

00:23:46.180 --> 00:23:51.150
because our police is
much more professional,

00:23:51.150 --> 00:23:55.120
because our judges are far more efficient,

00:23:55.120 --> 00:23:57.580
and because our prosecutors are better

00:23:57.580 --> 00:23:59.140
when they investigate,

00:23:59.140 --> 00:24:02.480
we have all these talents
in order to create

00:24:02.480 --> 00:24:07.480
a security matrix for the continent.

00:24:09.519 --> 00:24:13.324
The other existential
threat is corruption.

00:24:13.324 --> 00:24:18.123
And the conditions of
lack of transparency,

00:24:20.650 --> 00:24:23.710
definitely transparency are always there.

00:24:23.710 --> 00:24:28.710
We have not been able to overcome,

00:24:28.805 --> 00:24:33.805
maybe we are doing better
than in previous years,

00:24:33.810 --> 00:24:38.810
but more of the political
system is scrutinized.

00:24:39.640 --> 00:24:43.960
More of the political
system is investigated.

00:24:43.960 --> 00:24:45.650
And there are more persecutions

00:24:45.650 --> 00:24:48.150
related to corruption in public affairs.

00:24:48.150 --> 00:24:51.590
Nevertheless, we are far
from what it should be.

00:24:51.590 --> 00:24:56.050
And the fight of corruption
start to avoid in that,

00:24:56.050 --> 00:25:00.391
every government in action
is affected by that.

00:25:00.391 --> 00:25:04.810
It doesn't mean to indulge
the previous corruption,

00:25:04.810 --> 00:25:08.490
but it doesn't mean to persecute
the previous administration

00:25:08.490 --> 00:25:10.570
that it cannot, it can never work

00:25:10.570 --> 00:25:13.000
as a way of fighting corruption.

00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:15.020
Fighting corruption
should be a general thing,

00:25:15.020 --> 00:25:19.280
starting by yourself, by what
you do and the challenges

00:25:19.280 --> 00:25:21.183
that you have about your own moral

00:25:21.183 --> 00:25:25.053
and your Republican property
that you can deliver.

00:25:27.080 --> 00:25:29.210
And the fourth challenge is, of course,

00:25:29.210 --> 00:25:31.920
the fourth existential
threat are dictatorships,

00:25:31.920 --> 00:25:34.887
and we are having more
dictatorships, as I said,

00:25:34.887 --> 00:25:38.210
and they really affect because they bring

00:25:38.210 --> 00:25:41.360
all the bad practices to the hemisphere

00:25:41.360 --> 00:25:43.710
and to the political
systems of the hemisphere.

00:25:44.917 --> 00:25:48.540
Bad practices are very contagious,

00:25:48.540 --> 00:25:50.850
are very difficult to control even,

00:25:50.850 --> 00:25:55.850
to bring good practices
from one country to other,

00:25:56.400 --> 00:26:01.177
you need a project, you need
financing, you need experts.

00:26:01.177 --> 00:26:04.450
You need a lot of work and a program

00:26:04.450 --> 00:26:06.580
to execute that project.

00:26:06.580 --> 00:26:09.020
But practices, they have to spread

00:26:09.020 --> 00:26:11.900
without nobody saying anything,

00:26:11.900 --> 00:26:15.110
suddenly, you will see this
and this and this copying that,

00:26:15.110 --> 00:26:20.110
and starting this way of doing things.

00:26:20.110 --> 00:26:24.100
And that is why to have
dictatorships in the hemisphere

00:26:24.100 --> 00:26:28.701
is it's bringing a lot
of bacterial infections

00:26:28.701 --> 00:26:31.370
to our democracies.

00:26:31.370 --> 00:26:35.600
And I wish that one day
we can have a hemisphere

00:26:35.600 --> 00:26:40.600
free of dictatorships, free
of human rights violation,

00:26:41.900 --> 00:26:44.133
free of crimes against humanity,

00:26:45.340 --> 00:26:50.340
and that it will mean a step
forward in our development,

00:26:50.480 --> 00:26:53.650
mainly because this
dictatorships that you see them,

00:26:53.650 --> 00:26:56.330
they are mainly failed systems.

00:26:56.330 --> 00:27:00.470
They have failed completely in
social and production terms,

00:27:00.470 --> 00:27:02.690
and they are not, they have not been able.

00:27:02.690 --> 00:27:07.660
And even one of them is one
of the richest countries

00:27:07.660 --> 00:27:10.400
that exist in the world,
with a lot of oil,

00:27:10.400 --> 00:27:13.010
a lot of mineral
resources, a lot of energy,

00:27:13.010 --> 00:27:15.680
a lot of whatever you want in this world,

00:27:15.680 --> 00:27:20.120
and now is a broken country,
how will you create that?

00:27:20.120 --> 00:27:24.340
Because of a plan, a
formula that never worked.

00:27:24.340 --> 00:27:28.040
It is a formula of the
ideology of dictatorship.

00:27:28.040 --> 00:27:30.470
And this is not from, for
the right or for the left,

00:27:30.470 --> 00:27:32.880
this is the formula of a dictatorship.

00:27:32.880 --> 00:27:35.934
And that's why I started
saying in this hemisphere,

00:27:35.934 --> 00:27:38.740
what we see is that better democracies

00:27:38.740 --> 00:27:40.820
is better development conditions,

00:27:40.820 --> 00:27:45.820
better security conditions,
better access to right.

00:27:48.740 --> 00:27:53.740
We have a lot, a lot
ahead, and definitely,

00:27:58.360 --> 00:28:01.460
we need to address some of these issues

00:28:01.460 --> 00:28:06.460
because they are like
running out of control.

00:28:09.830 --> 00:28:11.613
Human rights violations,

00:28:14.230 --> 00:28:19.068
executives co-opt in the
judiciary, our weak parliaments

00:28:19.068 --> 00:28:21.341
and all these, it needs to be built

00:28:21.341 --> 00:28:25.050
if we want to be developed
countries at a certain moment,

00:28:25.050 --> 00:28:28.837
if we want to have better conditions

00:28:28.837 --> 00:28:33.837
for the living of
everybody in our societies.

00:28:34.120 --> 00:28:39.120
So it's, sometimes people think
this is a gloomy approach.

00:28:45.960 --> 00:28:48.140
And, I think, I say, I would never be able

00:28:48.140 --> 00:28:51.320
to resolve my problems
if I don't face them,

00:28:51.320 --> 00:28:52.790
if I don't identify them,

00:28:52.790 --> 00:28:55.770
I don't know which are these problems.

00:28:55.770 --> 00:28:58.210
That's why we have to start from here,

00:28:58.210 --> 00:29:02.090
if we want to have what the title says,

00:29:03.570 --> 00:29:07.143
it's translating democracy
for progress and peace.

00:29:07.143 --> 00:29:08.350
That's it.

00:29:08.350 --> 00:29:10.850
(crowd claps)

00:29:14.872 --> 00:29:15.955
- Sure, yeah.

00:29:26.380 --> 00:29:30.460
Hi everyone, as Claudia
mentioned, I'm Anna De La O,

00:29:30.460 --> 00:29:34.074
and thank you so much,
thank you so much to CLAIS.

00:29:34.074 --> 00:29:37.032
Wow, I don't even feel it anymore.

00:29:37.032 --> 00:29:39.030
(group laughs)

00:29:39.030 --> 00:29:42.060
Thank you so much to
CLAIS for the invitation

00:29:42.060 --> 00:29:46.160
to moderate this panel, and of course,

00:29:46.160 --> 00:29:49.610
it's an honor for me to be
here, Secretary General.

00:29:49.610 --> 00:29:54.370
Thank you so much for those
very thought provoking remarks.

00:29:54.370 --> 00:29:57.900
And you have already touched
on a few of the things

00:29:57.900 --> 00:30:01.900
that I was planning to ask you about,

00:30:01.900 --> 00:30:03.760
but I do want to start with the point

00:30:03.760 --> 00:30:08.760
about strengthening democracy,
because as both Claudia

00:30:10.010 --> 00:30:11.850
and you, Secretary General mentioned,

00:30:11.850 --> 00:30:13.945
the region has made great strides

00:30:13.945 --> 00:30:18.423
consolidating representative democracy,

00:30:18.423 --> 00:30:21.530
but there are signs of
democratic backsliding

00:30:21.530 --> 00:30:22.911
in some countries, of course,

00:30:22.911 --> 00:30:27.080
and the democratic
breakdown in some cases.

00:30:27.080 --> 00:30:30.100
And also according to
the last Latin American

00:30:30.100 --> 00:30:34.024
Public Opinion Project,
only 60% of Latin Americans

00:30:34.024 --> 00:30:36.480
would say that they prefer democracy

00:30:36.480 --> 00:30:41.480
to any other system of government,
which is quite alarming.

00:30:43.050 --> 00:30:46.505
And so I wonder, Secretary
General, whether we should,

00:30:46.505 --> 00:30:50.680
we have been focusing too
much on the role of elections

00:30:50.680 --> 00:30:53.660
and free and fair elections in the region,

00:30:53.660 --> 00:30:56.660
And whether you think
that there is now room

00:30:56.660 --> 00:30:58.920
to think about more direct forms

00:30:58.920 --> 00:31:03.920
of democratic participation,
and also your remarks

00:31:03.983 --> 00:31:07.470
make me think, when you
talked about polarization,

00:31:07.470 --> 00:31:12.320
inequality, security problems,
whether you think the region

00:31:12.320 --> 00:31:14.965
should be going in that direction or not

00:31:14.965 --> 00:31:17.933
to more direct forms of participation.

00:31:26.560 --> 00:31:31.363
- Yeah, first thing democracy
is, is a work for everybody.

00:31:34.630 --> 00:31:39.290
I mean, we have to start
from the very beginning,

00:31:39.290 --> 00:31:44.290
we have to be better citizens,
more tolerant citizens,

00:31:45.000 --> 00:31:50.000
and more flexible and more
looking at the perspective

00:31:50.100 --> 00:31:53.859
of what solutions we can
achieve and how we can build

00:31:53.859 --> 00:31:56.383
better things for our society.

00:32:00.102 --> 00:32:03.019
So I always hope that we can engage

00:32:05.780 --> 00:32:10.780
in better informing people
and being closer to the people

00:32:13.060 --> 00:32:16.610
in order to bring solutions in that,

00:32:17.490 --> 00:32:22.333
that may help everybody
to be a better citizen.

00:32:27.802 --> 00:32:30.557
Of course, this is not the only thing.

00:32:31.730 --> 00:32:34.590
I mean, we need to have
a stronger institution.

00:32:34.590 --> 00:32:39.010
So you were referring to
some service and polls

00:32:40.940 --> 00:32:45.067
that, of course, there is not so much

00:32:46.240 --> 00:32:49.453
of trust in democracy these days.

00:32:51.280 --> 00:32:55.890
but this makes me remember a phrase

00:32:57.012 --> 00:33:00.610
that my mother used to say,
that it was, (speaks Spanish).

00:33:06.077 --> 00:33:07.677
"You never know that you are fine

00:33:07.677 --> 00:33:09.727
"until you are not fine anymore."

00:33:10.821 --> 00:33:12.390
And we have seen that

00:33:13.290 --> 00:33:18.290
because those that they miss democracy,

00:33:19.650 --> 00:33:22.883
in Cuba, Venezuela,
those are in Nicaragua.

00:33:23.740 --> 00:33:27.050
They really want, they want democracy

00:33:27.050 --> 00:33:28.670
back in their countries.

00:33:28.670 --> 00:33:33.670
So those that they are not
so happy with democracy

00:33:34.010 --> 00:33:36.440
these days, it's because, of course,

00:33:36.440 --> 00:33:41.440
they are maybe more unhappy
about lack of efficiency

00:33:46.430 --> 00:33:51.370
of their government, or maybe not so good

00:33:52.440 --> 00:33:55.010
democratic governance itself.

00:33:55.010 --> 00:33:59.240
So that is the biggest
issue, but we can build

00:33:59.240 --> 00:34:03.080
a better democracy, also
building better institutions,

00:34:03.080 --> 00:34:05.030
we need better institutions these days.

00:34:05.030 --> 00:34:09.380
We need institutions that
can help us with solutions

00:34:09.380 --> 00:34:12.033
and can help us in real time.

00:34:13.280 --> 00:34:16.860
If you don't have institutions,
you stop helping people.

00:34:16.860 --> 00:34:20.920
If some countries, they
have a structural problems

00:34:20.920 --> 00:34:24.100
and the institutions are, have
been very weak for decades

00:34:24.100 --> 00:34:27.820
or maybe more, and you have some countries

00:34:27.820 --> 00:34:31.220
where the degradation of
institutions have been

00:34:31.220 --> 00:34:35.780
more of a gradual thing until
they are completely useless.

00:34:35.780 --> 00:34:40.227
And I don't like to mention it,

00:34:40.227 --> 00:34:43.740
but the case of Venezuela
is an example of that.

00:34:43.740 --> 00:34:46.590
Suddenly the Minister of Health

00:34:46.590 --> 00:34:51.003
cannot deliver solutions
in the health system,

00:34:52.160 --> 00:34:55.920
the Minister of Food, but cannot feed

00:34:55.920 --> 00:34:58.430
all the people of the country.

00:34:58.430 --> 00:35:02.780
The security, the Ministry of Interior

00:35:02.780 --> 00:35:07.100
cannot help people related
to security matters

00:35:07.100 --> 00:35:09.650
and not, they cannot even
protect the territory.

00:35:09.650 --> 00:35:13.480
I mean, the armed forces
of Venezuela went to fight

00:35:13.480 --> 00:35:16.372
the dissident of dissident
of FARC and Apure,

00:35:16.372 --> 00:35:21.372
and they took as his
heart punishment, beaten.

00:35:21.540 --> 00:35:24.790
So, not even they can control
the territory properly.

00:35:24.790 --> 00:35:27.050
So that is when you
deteriorate institutions

00:35:27.050 --> 00:35:30.086
to a level that you cannot help anybody,

00:35:30.086 --> 00:35:33.423
and you cannot deliver
solutions for anybody.

00:35:34.300 --> 00:35:37.073
So you need institutions
that can be efficient.

00:35:38.920 --> 00:35:42.430
The messianic approach is
of course a different way.

00:35:42.430 --> 00:35:47.430
I mean, the Messias, they don't
need a strong institutions,

00:35:48.500 --> 00:35:53.500
they come by themselves and
they resolve everything.

00:35:53.910 --> 00:35:57.040
But that, at the end, you see
that, that doesn't happen.

00:35:57.040 --> 00:36:00.913
And in the process, the
deterioration of institutions

00:36:00.913 --> 00:36:04.993
can bring the country to
a new level of problems.

00:36:07.060 --> 00:36:11.517
If we are ready to more
direct forms of democracy,

00:36:13.670 --> 00:36:17.640
we are already doing more
direct forms of democracy.

00:36:17.640 --> 00:36:22.640
We are participating every day
in politics, wherever we are.

00:36:26.766 --> 00:36:31.766
And wherever you're based
and whatever is your country,

00:36:34.980 --> 00:36:37.940
you are able to participate
in the political life there

00:36:37.940 --> 00:36:41.160
because you have a Twitter,
a Facebook account,

00:36:41.160 --> 00:36:44.720
and then you can really create conditions

00:36:44.720 --> 00:36:45.900
for public opinion,

00:36:45.900 --> 00:36:50.090
and you can bring new issues
to the political system.

00:36:50.090 --> 00:36:53.133
And it's a new way of participating,

00:36:53.133 --> 00:36:55.530
it's a much more direct one.

00:36:55.530 --> 00:36:59.820
And I agree with that,
but that doesn't resolve

00:37:05.560 --> 00:37:07.120
everything that democracy needs.

00:37:07.120 --> 00:37:10.850
I mean, as I said, we need to face

00:37:10.850 --> 00:37:12.620
the problem of inequality.

00:37:12.620 --> 00:37:16.800
We need to resolve the
problems of security.

00:37:16.800 --> 00:37:19.240
So we need to create conditions

00:37:19.240 --> 00:37:21.829
to better produce in the country.

00:37:21.829 --> 00:37:25.995
And that, it cannot be done
only chatting in Twitter

00:37:25.995 --> 00:37:30.995
or in a social media, there
you have to really work.

00:37:31.230 --> 00:37:33.110
And sometimes the people forget

00:37:33.110 --> 00:37:35.210
that politics is working, its service.

00:37:35.210 --> 00:37:38.817
I mean, you serve people
and you serve people

00:37:38.817 --> 00:37:42.260
with a purpose of making
your society better,

00:37:42.260 --> 00:37:44.170
but you have to do things.

00:37:44.170 --> 00:37:46.500
You have to be a doer, you have to do,

00:37:46.500 --> 00:37:49.473
if you only want to talk about politics,

00:37:50.420 --> 00:37:53.190
yeah, maybe the social media will end

00:37:53.190 --> 00:37:58.170
only replacing our very
serious conversations

00:37:58.170 --> 00:38:01.310
that we had in our bars
and cafes in Latin America,

00:38:01.310 --> 00:38:04.420
but will not hardly be
enough to solve our problems.

00:38:04.420 --> 00:38:09.420
- Mm-hm, yeah, and so,
since you were now talking

00:38:09.770 --> 00:38:13.200
about inequality, so that seems to be

00:38:13.200 --> 00:38:18.200
a very big structural ancient
problem in Latin America.

00:38:19.130 --> 00:38:22.860
There was a point in time
in the 2010s, more or less,

00:38:22.860 --> 00:38:25.450
where it seems that some
countries had experienced

00:38:25.450 --> 00:38:27.600
a small decrease in inequality.

00:38:27.600 --> 00:38:29.960
And people started getting very excited

00:38:29.960 --> 00:38:32.700
about seeing a change in
the trend in inequality.

00:38:32.700 --> 00:38:35.580
But of course, even before the pandemic,

00:38:35.580 --> 00:38:39.143
that reason for hope was already,

00:38:41.220 --> 00:38:43.090
we didn't have enough reason to hope

00:38:43.090 --> 00:38:46.140
that the trend was going to
continue in that direction.

00:38:46.140 --> 00:38:49.280
So I'm just wondering,
you mentioned corruption,

00:38:49.280 --> 00:38:52.460
you mentioned inequality,
you mentioned discrimination.

00:38:52.460 --> 00:38:57.210
It all points to perhaps
a broken social contract

00:38:57.210 --> 00:38:58.490
in many of these countries,

00:38:58.490 --> 00:39:00.380
that it's a very complicated problem

00:39:00.380 --> 00:39:05.380
because if governments don't
have access to resources,

00:39:05.390 --> 00:39:08.510
so if tax revenues are low,
people don't want to pay taxes,

00:39:08.510 --> 00:39:10.100
people don't trust that other people

00:39:10.100 --> 00:39:12.003
are going to want to pay taxes.

00:39:13.586 --> 00:39:16.820
It sort of drops countries
in a negative equilibrium,

00:39:16.820 --> 00:39:20.140
in a way, in a very negative cycle,

00:39:20.140 --> 00:39:23.960
I'm just wondering how to get out of it?

00:39:23.960 --> 00:39:27.170
And I wonder whether
organizations like the OAS

00:39:27.170 --> 00:39:30.210
has, sort of a vision of how to promote,

00:39:30.210 --> 00:39:33.759
maybe tax agreements, I don't know exactly

00:39:33.759 --> 00:39:36.400
what would be the way forward?

00:39:36.400 --> 00:39:39.140
And is there room for
international cooperation

00:39:39.140 --> 00:39:41.803
to get the country moving
in the right direction?

00:39:43.141 --> 00:39:45.717
- International cooperation
is always necessary,

00:39:45.717 --> 00:39:50.717
and you always have to
refer to some countries,

00:39:55.368 --> 00:39:58.120
some states, some
nations that can be doing

00:39:58.120 --> 00:40:01.163
the things in the right way.

00:40:03.640 --> 00:40:07.740
So international
cooperation, and you may need

00:40:07.740 --> 00:40:11.100
the assistance and the
cooperation of these countries

00:40:11.100 --> 00:40:14.450
in order to better do a couple of things

00:40:14.450 --> 00:40:17.643
that you want to do in
your country, my country.

00:40:21.670 --> 00:40:24.320
Yeah, I was part of a government

00:40:24.320 --> 00:40:28.910
that reduced inequality
as much as we could,

00:40:28.910 --> 00:40:33.580
and we reduced poverty
to one digit in Uruguay.

00:40:37.600 --> 00:40:42.600
And that is, it can be
done, it can be done.

00:40:43.520 --> 00:40:46.567
And sometimes people refer to Mujica,

00:40:46.567 --> 00:40:50.733
and they prefer a lot more,
his philosophical part.

00:40:52.440 --> 00:40:55.680
I highly appreciate more
his professionalism,

00:40:55.680 --> 00:40:58.320
he was a professional doing his job,

00:40:58.320 --> 00:41:02.100
he worked from very early in the morning.

00:41:02.100 --> 00:41:03.870
I was sure that if I would,

00:41:03.870 --> 00:41:06.346
I was having a call half
past six in the morning,

00:41:06.346 --> 00:41:08.010
it would come from him.

00:41:08.010 --> 00:41:12.170
And I was sure that if I
was getting a call at 11

00:41:12.170 --> 00:41:14.700
in the evening, it would be from him

00:41:14.700 --> 00:41:18.400
and that he would have
20 meetings in a day,

00:41:18.400 --> 00:41:22.930
and that he would be trying to deal

00:41:22.930 --> 00:41:24.880
with practically every single matter

00:41:24.880 --> 00:41:26.710
that exists in the country.

00:41:26.710 --> 00:41:30.942
Why we have very few, so you
can do a lot of micromanagement

00:41:30.942 --> 00:41:33.420
when even you are, at the same time,

00:41:33.420 --> 00:41:35.700
you can be doing high politics

00:41:35.700 --> 00:41:38.650
and it can be done, it can be done.

00:41:38.650 --> 00:41:41.940
And you have to have the
purpose of that, of course.

00:41:41.940 --> 00:41:45.210
You have to have the purpose of bringing

00:41:45.210 --> 00:41:47.450
more equal conditions to your people.

00:41:47.450 --> 00:41:50.370
If you don't have that
purpose, you won't do it,

00:41:50.370 --> 00:41:52.160
but even if you work a lot.

00:41:52.160 --> 00:41:57.160
But that is a thing, you
have to bring these solutions

00:41:58.695 --> 00:42:03.695
and it's possible to
achieve, and it can be done.

00:42:08.170 --> 00:42:11.233
And of course, and that
is what I mean when,

00:42:12.160 --> 00:42:15.510
but you have to work the
things in the long term,

00:42:15.510 --> 00:42:19.335
it is not enough for, to
work for a couple of years,

00:42:19.335 --> 00:42:22.220
to work for 10 years,
to work for 20 years.

00:42:22.220 --> 00:42:25.120
I mean, we are in a stage that we need

00:42:25.120 --> 00:42:29.883
to work very hard for longer than that.

00:42:31.500 --> 00:42:33.305
If our social contract is broken,

00:42:33.305 --> 00:42:38.200
I think our social, the
social contract that we have,

00:42:38.200 --> 00:42:41.500
and that's why I mention
inequality, discrimination,

00:42:41.500 --> 00:42:45.417
paternalistic, et cetera,
et cetera, et cetera,

00:42:46.430 --> 00:42:48.833
it was a failed social contract.

00:42:48.833 --> 00:42:51.410
Maybe it's not broken,
and that is a problem,

00:42:51.410 --> 00:42:52.243
maybe they are trying,

00:42:52.243 --> 00:42:56.020
they have some people trying
to hold to that old contract

00:42:56.020 --> 00:43:01.020
that he has 200 years or 150
years, but we definitely,

00:43:01.500 --> 00:43:04.600
we cannot keep going with
that social contract,

00:43:04.600 --> 00:43:07.810
we need a more efficient social contract.

00:43:07.810 --> 00:43:12.210
We need a social contract that can break

00:43:12.210 --> 00:43:17.120
with these structures,
and that can help us

00:43:17.120 --> 00:43:22.120
to bring some of the real
solutions that I have mentioned.

00:43:22.660 --> 00:43:27.660
So, the problem is that,
maybe is that it's not broken,

00:43:29.990 --> 00:43:34.990
but it's a failed one,
it took us for 200 years

00:43:35.230 --> 00:43:37.980
without delivering the proper solutions

00:43:37.980 --> 00:43:40.000
and it's still there and it's still,

00:43:40.000 --> 00:43:41.240
we have to deal with that.

00:43:41.240 --> 00:43:42.730
And that is the biggest problem.

00:43:42.730 --> 00:43:47.730
So we need a better social contract.

00:43:48.480 --> 00:43:49.500
With another problem,

00:43:49.500 --> 00:43:52.597
no, I mean, we don't socialize well,

00:44:00.880 --> 00:44:05.833
our socialization also
is not an efficient one,

00:44:07.270 --> 00:44:09.350
It is not the right one.

00:44:09.350 --> 00:44:13.000
You could see it during COVID
times, you could see it.

00:44:13.000 --> 00:44:17.230
I mean, and you could see it
in a practically every country.

00:44:17.230 --> 00:44:19.230
Sometimes you have very strict measures

00:44:19.230 --> 00:44:22.720
of quarantine and all this.

00:44:22.720 --> 00:44:27.260
suddenly, but you could
see that at the same time,

00:44:27.260 --> 00:44:31.040
they were having, people
having clandestine parties

00:44:31.040 --> 00:44:35.360
of 500 people, and we were
people having clandestine party

00:44:35.360 --> 00:44:40.250
for less than that, but they
were, and that is the thing,

00:44:40.250 --> 00:44:43.566
I mean, we were not, we
don't have this thing that

00:44:43.566 --> 00:44:47.910
it may exist here and I think
existed, but I don't know

00:44:47.910 --> 00:44:50.050
so much of places in the city,

00:44:50.050 --> 00:44:54.505
but, in your way, somehow
existed this community life

00:44:54.505 --> 00:44:57.380
that exist here, that
people are responsible

00:44:57.380 --> 00:45:00.240
for the neighbor, for what
is going on with the street,

00:45:00.240 --> 00:45:02.560
with your sidewalk, with your tree,

00:45:02.560 --> 00:45:07.340
with everything is how you
have better community life.

00:45:07.340 --> 00:45:10.110
And that is means how you socialize better

00:45:10.110 --> 00:45:15.110
in a now, a wider view of
solutions that maybe better,

00:45:18.470 --> 00:45:20.240
but the start, a lot of things that start

00:45:20.240 --> 00:45:25.240
by you, by you trying to
do things in a better way,

00:45:25.270 --> 00:45:27.000
but socializing better.

00:45:27.000 --> 00:45:29.440
If you just try to avoid the rules,

00:45:29.440 --> 00:45:32.840
avoid the norms, trying
to create conditions

00:45:32.840 --> 00:45:34.970
that where you can take some advantages,

00:45:34.970 --> 00:45:37.750
but you don't take the
obligations and the duties,

00:45:37.750 --> 00:45:39.864
the responsibilities that comes with that

00:45:39.864 --> 00:45:41.910
will be very, very difficult.

00:45:41.910 --> 00:45:43.860
And that is another problem that we have.

00:45:43.860 --> 00:45:48.670
So, we have this problem of
the failed social contract,

00:45:48.670 --> 00:45:51.520
but we also have the problem of that,

00:45:51.520 --> 00:45:53.530
the wrong way we socialize.

00:45:53.530 --> 00:45:55.720
- [Maria] Mm-hm, Secretary General,

00:45:55.720 --> 00:45:57.150
I think that we, at this point,

00:45:57.150 --> 00:46:00.080
might open the floor for
questions from the audience,

00:46:00.080 --> 00:46:02.490
if there is one.

00:46:02.490 --> 00:46:04.775
- I have been so clear, I don't
think they have questions.

00:46:04.775 --> 00:46:08.090
(crowd laughs)

00:46:08.090 --> 00:46:08.923
- Yeah?

00:46:10.690 --> 00:46:12.920
- [Student] Hello, so earlier today,

00:46:12.920 --> 00:46:14.870
you talked about how
platforms like Facebook--

00:46:14.870 --> 00:46:16.963
- Can you please, because...

00:46:16.963 --> 00:46:17.796
- [Student] The mask?

00:46:17.796 --> 00:46:18.629
- Yeah, please.

00:46:18.629 --> 00:46:20.230
- [Student] You always say
you talk about platforms

00:46:20.230 --> 00:46:22.800
like Facebook can be used
to express public opinion

00:46:22.800 --> 00:46:25.840
and used to kind of promote
like democratic ideals.

00:46:25.840 --> 00:46:28.120
Also, we see how platforms like Facebook

00:46:28.120 --> 00:46:30.600
can promote like hyper polarization

00:46:30.600 --> 00:46:32.150
also can spread misinformation,

00:46:32.150 --> 00:46:34.930
which can lead democratic backsliding.

00:46:34.930 --> 00:46:36.280
I was wondering whether or not you thought

00:46:36.280 --> 00:46:39.620
that kinda like regulating
these social media platforms

00:46:39.620 --> 00:46:42.360
should be tackled by
international community,

00:46:42.360 --> 00:46:45.160
or something to be handled
domestically by each country?

00:46:49.142 --> 00:46:51.177
- You know what, first of all,

00:46:53.125 --> 00:46:58.125
if you have common problems,
try to resolve them together.

00:46:59.060 --> 00:47:03.970
Because if not, you will
only have a cockroach effect.

00:47:03.970 --> 00:47:06.117
I mean, you will stop it somewhere

00:47:06.117 --> 00:47:09.223
and it will just move somewhere else.

00:47:10.750 --> 00:47:15.750
And so, I think, I was very
tempted to whatever it works,

00:47:18.048 --> 00:47:22.880
and of course there are
some levels of the things

00:47:22.880 --> 00:47:24.460
that they need to address,

00:47:24.460 --> 00:47:27.230
to be addressed at the national level,

00:47:27.230 --> 00:47:30.760
because of course, every
country has different scenarios

00:47:30.760 --> 00:47:32.810
and they start from different points.

00:47:32.810 --> 00:47:37.790
And their social, their
freedom of expression,

00:47:37.790 --> 00:47:39.434
or social media or whatever,

00:47:39.434 --> 00:47:42.743
is completely different than the country

00:47:42.743 --> 00:47:46.630
a couple of thousands of kilometers away.

00:47:46.630 --> 00:47:49.930
So there are specificities
that will be needed

00:47:49.930 --> 00:47:52.970
to progress at the national level,

00:47:52.970 --> 00:47:56.590
but we need to see the common problems

00:47:56.590 --> 00:48:01.530
that these generate and
how we can help each other

00:48:01.530 --> 00:48:06.387
in order to avoid things that can come

00:48:07.390 --> 00:48:10.392
with this digitalization of politics.

00:48:10.392 --> 00:48:15.392
And you see permanently these days,

00:48:17.100 --> 00:48:19.940
attacks, digital attacks coming

00:48:19.940 --> 00:48:22.650
to your country from where
ever else in the world.

00:48:22.650 --> 00:48:25.160
And that is something, of course,

00:48:25.160 --> 00:48:27.810
that we need to have
a better understanding

00:48:27.810 --> 00:48:32.810
of how our digital international
relations should be.

00:48:33.930 --> 00:48:37.220
I mean, it cannot be a jungle.

00:48:37.220 --> 00:48:41.857
It cannot be like
everybody trying to prevail

00:48:41.857 --> 00:48:45.285
with the world tools against others,

00:48:45.285 --> 00:48:47.700
but how we can be helpful in order

00:48:47.700 --> 00:48:50.733
to create a better
international community.

00:48:52.180 --> 00:48:57.180
And of course, any rules that
you bring into the system,

00:48:57.888 --> 00:49:01.730
you have to be, designed
them extremely carefully,

00:49:01.730 --> 00:49:05.653
because, I mean, any drop that falls,

00:49:08.217 --> 00:49:13.217
any drop of ink that falls
into the freedom of expression,

00:49:13.302 --> 00:49:17.580
it can be a serious problem,
it will be a serious problem.

00:49:17.580 --> 00:49:21.750
So how you can manage
that in order to create

00:49:21.750 --> 00:49:24.203
even better conditions
for freedom of expression.

00:49:26.205 --> 00:49:30.140
And I am one of the most
insulted people in media.

00:49:30.140 --> 00:49:32.820
You know, all my
adversaries, they have trolls

00:49:32.820 --> 00:49:35.230
and farms of trolls all around.

00:49:35.230 --> 00:49:39.481
I only have my humble
account and I can only say

00:49:39.481 --> 00:49:44.296
the most evident truth,
because if one day I lie,

00:49:44.296 --> 00:49:45.253
I will be killed.

00:49:49.586 --> 00:49:53.440
So, as one of the most
insulted person in media,

00:49:53.440 --> 00:49:55.910
I can tell you that it's good

00:49:55.910 --> 00:49:57.873
to keep freedom of expression,

00:49:57.873 --> 00:50:01.080
and to assure that by all means.

00:50:01.080 --> 00:50:05.550
And I don't have any problem
with anonymous insulting me.

00:50:05.550 --> 00:50:09.030
I don't have a problem with
that, but I may have a problem

00:50:09.030 --> 00:50:11.020
with anonymous committing crimes

00:50:11.020 --> 00:50:12.810
like pedophilia or something like that.

00:50:12.810 --> 00:50:17.160
So those things, anonymity
cannot be protected

00:50:17.160 --> 00:50:19.890
in those cases where you offend the law.

00:50:19.890 --> 00:50:24.130
But of course, and I consider
that insulting a politician

00:50:24.130 --> 00:50:25.350
is freedom of expression.

00:50:25.350 --> 00:50:30.290
So it's where you do that,
and that can be assured.

00:50:30.290 --> 00:50:32.963
- [Maria] Mm-hm, yes?

00:50:37.617 --> 00:50:39.480
- Good afternoon, My
name is Eka Khorbaladze,

00:50:39.480 --> 00:50:41.710
Fox International fellow (mumbles)

00:50:41.710 --> 00:50:43.397
First of all, thank you
very much Secretary General

00:50:43.397 --> 00:50:45.720
for your time and very interesting talk.

00:50:45.720 --> 00:50:47.509
If you let me, I'd have
two parts question.

00:50:47.509 --> 00:50:50.860
The first is, you spoke
a lot about democracy

00:50:50.860 --> 00:50:54.110
and its importance, and
I wonder, of course,

00:50:54.110 --> 00:50:56.939
I agree with that this
is very important value

00:50:56.939 --> 00:51:00.100
in which it's possible
to build good government

00:51:01.230 --> 00:51:06.230
to develop rules in countries

00:51:06.680 --> 00:51:08.460
and in integrational processes.

00:51:08.460 --> 00:51:10.387
But, I wonder if we take into account

00:51:10.387 --> 00:51:12.858
the example of China and we'll look

00:51:12.858 --> 00:51:15.100
how it becomes the great power,

00:51:15.100 --> 00:51:17.343
how its economy is growing so accordingly,

00:51:18.370 --> 00:51:22.070
the people's condition become better,

00:51:22.070 --> 00:51:23.670
living conditions become better,

00:51:24.829 --> 00:51:29.640
how we can, and taking
into account what you said

00:51:29.640 --> 00:51:32.520
before about democracy, compare it

00:51:32.520 --> 00:51:34.230
with what is happening in China.

00:51:34.230 --> 00:51:39.230
And the second part of my
question is don't you think

00:51:40.020 --> 00:51:45.020
that such kind of building
integration processes

00:51:46.870 --> 00:51:49.970
on such value as democracy
reduces the capability

00:51:49.970 --> 00:51:52.170
of organization to other countries,

00:51:52.170 --> 00:51:56.699
which I would say don't
have democratic regimes?

00:51:56.699 --> 00:51:57.532
Thanks a lot.

00:51:59.037 --> 00:52:03.240
- Yeah, and I, of course
I will ask you to do so,

00:52:03.240 --> 00:52:08.097
I have always said, democracy is the best

00:52:14.620 --> 00:52:18.080
in this hemisphere for
development, security

00:52:18.080 --> 00:52:19.470
in this hemisphere.

00:52:19.470 --> 00:52:24.040
I can not go far beyond my jurisdiction,

00:52:24.040 --> 00:52:27.620
as you may realize, in any case,

00:52:27.620 --> 00:52:31.283
every country has different
tools and different approaches.

00:52:34.219 --> 00:52:38.030
I served as ambassador
of Uruguay to China.

00:52:38.030 --> 00:52:43.030
And of course, I saw the
process of a developing country

00:52:45.620 --> 00:52:48.500
that, and you could see that every day

00:52:48.500 --> 00:52:51.180
they were really economically growing

00:52:51.180 --> 00:52:56.080
and economically bringing better
conditions for the people,

00:52:56.080 --> 00:52:58.060
and socially bringing better conditions

00:52:58.060 --> 00:53:00.730
for the people day by day.

00:53:00.730 --> 00:53:04.064
And practically, every Chinese
person was very optimistic

00:53:04.064 --> 00:53:07.867
that in the next year, he would be better

00:53:07.867 --> 00:53:12.547
than this year and that
has been quite the reason

00:53:16.010 --> 00:53:18.623
for an optimist for quite a while.

00:53:22.460 --> 00:53:27.284
They have a completely
collective pattern of working.

00:53:27.284 --> 00:53:31.863
And I mean, it's for
them collective action.

00:53:34.920 --> 00:53:39.260
And this, be a spirit of
doing the things together,

00:53:39.260 --> 00:53:41.960
the enhance spirit of
doing the things together

00:53:41.960 --> 00:53:44.040
is a completely different one

00:53:44.040 --> 00:53:49.040
that we have in this hemisphere,
western hemisphere so far.

00:53:49.420 --> 00:53:54.420
So, of course, when we talk about systems,

00:54:00.310 --> 00:54:05.310
I don't think you can never
go to an agreement of any kind

00:54:05.530 --> 00:54:07.530
where you resign your liberties,

00:54:07.530 --> 00:54:12.060
your fundamental freedoms,
your democratic rule of law.

00:54:14.150 --> 00:54:17.520
You can't resign that, you
have to keep it with you

00:54:17.520 --> 00:54:19.333
because that will be a bigger loss

00:54:19.333 --> 00:54:22.861
than anything that can happen.

00:54:22.861 --> 00:54:27.861
And, of course, I always
hope that in that process,

00:54:31.370 --> 00:54:33.740
more fundamental freedoms
will be available

00:54:33.740 --> 00:54:38.740
for Chinese people and
that they may develop

00:54:39.300 --> 00:54:41.420
and evolve in that direction.

00:54:41.420 --> 00:54:44.010
And that is an imperative that, of course,

00:54:44.010 --> 00:54:46.920
we always have to have in mind,

00:54:46.920 --> 00:54:51.580
but, what I say is that we
cannot lose what we have

00:54:51.580 --> 00:54:54.750
about fundamental freedoms,
because if we do that,

00:54:54.750 --> 00:54:57.770
we are going to really
miss them, I can tell you,

00:54:57.770 --> 00:54:59.670
we are going to really miss them.

00:54:59.670 --> 00:55:04.573
And in this continent, the closer,

00:55:05.680 --> 00:55:07.713
the better our democracies work,

00:55:08.720 --> 00:55:11.440
the better our conditions
for access to right,

00:55:11.440 --> 00:55:13.380
for security and for development,

00:55:13.380 --> 00:55:17.803
that is a fact in this continent,
in this continent type.

00:55:19.327 --> 00:55:22.640
- Secretary General, we are out of time,

00:55:22.640 --> 00:55:25.700
but I want to thank you
so much on behalf of CLAIS

00:55:25.700 --> 00:55:27.900
for taking the time, especially now,

00:55:27.900 --> 00:55:30.840
considering the
circumstances to talk to us,

00:55:30.840 --> 00:55:31.724
thank you so much.

00:55:31.724 --> 00:55:32.557
(speaks Spanish)

00:55:32.557 --> 00:55:34.087
(laughs)

00:55:34.087 --> 00:55:36.587
(crowd claps)

00:55:37.955 --> 00:55:38.788
- Thank You

00:55:38.788 --> 00:55:40.455
- Thank you so much.

